    "verdict": "Norris is the model pick. He won yesterday's Sprint and has the fastest race-trim pace by about 0.2 s/lap over Leclerc. Starting P4 is a real but manageable handicap. Even under aggressive overtaking-difficulty assumptions (tested 0 to 2.5 s/grid-spot) he stays the favourite at 33 to 40%. Antonelli has track position but his Sprint pace dropped him to 4th-fastest under heat. Hedge: Leclerc for podium.",
    "methodology": "We pull all FP/Sprint/Qualifying data via FastF1, estimate clean-air race-trim pace from yesterday's Sprint (best 60% of laps, excluding in/out/SC laps), then run 30,000 Monte Carlo race simulations. Each driver's per-lap time is sampled around their measured pace with observed variance plus a race-noise term. Track position is modelled as a per-grid-spot time penalty plus a dirty-air cost when faster cars sit behind slower ones (Miami is a hard-to-overtake circuit). Team-tier DNF rates are applied. Win probability is the share of simulations won."
